Chapter: GopWorld
  cites Works chapter GopDraw
  cites Works chapter GopComposite
  cites Works chapter GopZones
  cites Works chapter GopLocale

 The world, coarsely, and the 24 bands drawn over it.

 Stage 4 of `docs/Designs/Active/Apps/ClockAndZone.md`.

 THIS IS A PICTURE AND NOT A PROJECTION ENGINE. Nothing here computes a
 Mercator from coordinates. The landmasses are 72 rows of 144 columns, hand
 traced, and the only geometry is that column x is longitude and row y is
 latitude on the same linear scale a Mercator uses for longitude. A click
 picks a STRIPE by its column, never a country by its shape, which is the
 whole reason a coarse picture is enough.

 THE ROWS SPAN 80 DEGREES NORTH TO 60 SOUTH, which is where a real Mercator
 stops being usable: the projection sends the poles to infinity, so every
 published one is cropped and this one is cropped in the same place. It costs
 nothing, because nobody picks a timezone by clicking Antarctica.

Section: The Grid

  wld-cols : Integer = 144
  wld-rows : Integer = 72

 Each row is 144 bits as nine 16-bit words, most significant bit first, which
 is the shape that fits an Integer literal per word and stays readable as a
 map when the words are laid out in a column. Sea is 0 and land is 1.

  wld-words : Integer = 9

  wld-cells : Integer = 648

 THE TABLE IS COPIED INTO A BLOCK ONCE AND INDEXED AFTER THAT. `wld-bit-at` is
 a chain of 367 comparisons, which is the shape a generated table takes in a
 language with no array literal. Read per pixel it would cost about four
 million comparisons to draw one map, and the pane redraws every second. Built
 into 648 words at open and read with `peek-32`, it costs one load.

 The caller owns the block and passes it back in, the way every other pane on
 this desk owns its state: allocated once above the desk's base mark, pointer
 kept in a cell, never rebuilt inside a frame bracket.

  wld-fill : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wld-fill (b) (i) =
    if i >= wld-cells then 0
    else let w = poke-32 b (i * 4) (wld-bit-at i)
    in wld-fill b (i + 1)

  wld-build : Integer
  wld-build =
    let b = alloc-zeroed (wld-cells * 4) 4
    in let f = wld-fill b 0
    in b

  wld-word : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wld-word (blob) (y) (w) = peek-32 blob ((y * wld-words + w) * 4)

  wld-land : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  wld-land (blob) (x) (y) =
    if blob == 0 then False
    else if x < 0 then False
    else if x >= wld-cols then False
    else if y < 0 then False
    else if y >= wld-rows then False
    else bit-and (wld-word blob y (x / 16)) (bit-shl 1 (15 - int-mod x 16)) /= 0

Section: Stripes

 THE BAND IS THE COLUMN AND THE COLUMN IS THE LONGITUDE. 144 columns over 360
 degrees is six columns per hour, and the map is centred so that column 72 is
 the prime meridian: stripe s spans columns `72 + s * 6` to `72 + s * 6 + 6`.

 Stripes run -12 to +11 across the picture, which is 24 bands and covers the
 world once. The table has zones at +12, +13 and +14, which are east of the
 date line and are drawn in the SAME columns as -12, -11 and -10. That is not
 a bug in the picture, it is what the date line is, and the list beside the
 map is what separates them.

  wld-stripe-cols : Integer = 6

  wld-stripe-x0 : Integer -> Integer
  wld-stripe-x0 (s) = 72 + s * wld-stripe-cols

  wld-col-stripe : Integer -> Integer
  wld-col-stripe (x) =
    let c = (x - 72) / wld-stripe-cols
    in if x < 72 & int-mod (x - 72) wld-stripe-cols /= 0 then c - 1 else c

Section: Drawing

 The map is drawn at an integer SCALE so that a column is a whole number of
 device pixels and the bands line up with the land. A fractional scale would
 put a band edge inside a pixel and the stripe under the pointer would not be
 the stripe that was drawn there.

  wld-scale-for : Integer -> Integer
  wld-scale-for (w) = let s = w / wld-cols in if s < 1 then 1 else s

  wld-cell : Integer, Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wld-cell (base) (stride) (rows) (clip) (px) (py) (sc) (col) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else
      let d = comp-fill base stride rows clip (px + int-mod i sc) (py + i / sc) 1 1 col
      in wld-cell base stride rows clip px py sc col (i + 1) n

  wld-row-at : Integer, Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wld-row-at (base) (stride) (rows) (clip) (blob) (ox) (oy) (sc) (y) (x) (sea) (land) (sel) =
    if x >= wld-cols then 0
    else
      let on = wld-land blob x y
      in let inband = sel >= (0 - 12) & wld-col-stripe x == sel
      in let c = if on then (if inband then wld-sel-land else land) else (if inband then wld-sel-sea else sea)
      in let d = comp-fill base stride rows clip (ox + x * sc) (oy + y * sc) sc sc c
      in wld-row-at base stride rows clip blob ox oy sc y (x + 1) sea land sel

  wld-rows-at : Integer, Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wld-rows-at (base) (stride) (rows) (clip) (blob) (ox) (oy) (sc) (y) (sea) (land) (sel) =
    if y >= wld-rows then 0
    else
      let one = wld-row-at base stride rows clip blob ox oy sc y 0 sea land sel
      in wld-rows-at base stride rows clip blob ox oy sc (y + 1) sea land sel

 The selected band is a WASH over both sea and land rather than a frame around
 them, because a frame six columns wide is two vertical lines and reads as two
 lines rather than as a band. The land inside it stays distinguishable from
 the sea inside it, which is what makes the selection legible instead of a
 solid bar across the picture.

  wld-sel-sea : Integer = 3372975
  wld-sel-land : Integer = 6750105

 GRID LINES EVERY THREE HOURS, so a reader can count bands without tracing
 each one. Every third stripe boundary gets a line, which puts one on the
 prime meridian and one on the date line, the two a reader looks for first.

  wld-grid-at : Integer, Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wld-grid-at (base) (stride) (rows) (clip) (ox) (oy) (sc) (s) (hi) (col) =
    if s > hi then 0
    else
      let x = wld-stripe-x0 s
      in let d = if int-mod (s + 12) 3 == 0 & x >= 0 & x < wld-cols then comp-fill base stride rows clip (ox + x * sc) oy 1 (wld-rows * sc) col else 0
      in wld-grid-at base stride rows clip ox oy sc (s + 1) hi col

  wld-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wld-draw (base) (stride) (rows) (clip) (blob) (ox) (oy) (sc) (sea) (land) (grid) (sel) =
    let m = wld-rows-at base stride rows clip blob ox oy sc 0 sea land sel
    in wld-grid-at base stride rows clip ox oy sc (0 - 12) 11 grid

Section: Hitting

 The inverse of the drawing, and it takes the same origin and scale so the two
 cannot drift. A point outside the picture answers a stripe of -99 rather than
 clamping to an edge: a click past the map is not a click on its last band,
 and treating it as one puts somebody in Kiritimati for missing.

  wld-none : Integer = 0 - 99

  wld-hit-stripe : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wld-hit-stripe (ox) (oy) (sc) (mx) (my) (unused) =
    let x = (mx - ox) / sc
    in let y = (my - oy) / sc
    in if mx < ox then wld-none
    else if my < oy then wld-none
    else if x >= wld-cols then wld-none
    else if y >= wld-rows then wld-none
    else wld-col-stripe x

Section: The Table

 GENERATED, and the generator is not in the depot because its OUTPUT is the
 artifact: 367 non-empty words rasterised from about twenty coastline polygons
 in latitude and longitude. Editing a coastline here means editing these
 numbers, which is the honest cost of a picture in a language with no array
 literal, and the picture is checked by looking at it rather than by reading
 them.

